From faef93a93d8f771cfe7de0b86db942d032a051af Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: loudness-agent <agent@example.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 15:01:51 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 01/10] feat(loudness): whole-track loudness normalization (one
 fixed gain per track)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

B站 does not hand its loudness metadata to third party players, so measure it
locally instead of falling back to ffmpeg's whole-file replaygain:

  * sidx sampled measurement: read only the fMP4 index plus 12 evenly spread
    ~5s fragments (~0.5MB, <1% of a long video), decode each fragment as
    `init + ONE moof` separately, then run ITU-R BS.1770-4 (K weighting, 400ms
    blocks, absolute + relative gates) to get integrated loudness and true peak
  * one constant gain per track, applied through
    `<audio> -> MediaElementAudioSourceNode -> GainNode -> destination` with a
    120ms ramp. No compressor, no limiter, dynamics untouched. Per the Web
    Audio spec the element's own volume keeps working, so the gain no longer
    fights the volume slider or the saved default volume
  * gain = profile target LUFS - measured LUFS, bounded by "true peak <= -1
    dBTP" (boosts only; attenuation is never limited) and the profile caps
  * six presets (standard -14 / dynamic -24 / headphone -16 / speaker -11 /
    night -20 / custom) plus sliders, mirroring the Bilibili_LoudNorm userscript
  * measurements are cached per cid in pglite (new `loudness-table`): changing a
    target or a preset never re-measures
  * degradation chain: GainNode -> element volume (attenuation only) -> original
    audio. Live streams are skipped, as before
  * the next queue item is measured while the current one plays, so switching
    between two very different videos does not jump

Also keeps the legacy `r128gain` switch working (untouched code path), makes the
ffmpeg replaygain usable as a selectable engine (dynamic import, so the wasm is
not loaded unless it is used), and fixes the sign of B站's gain metadata in
utils/ffmpeg/r128util.ts.

Algorithm ported from Bilibili_LoudNorm (MIT, (c) 2026 Moxia9527); every ported
file keeps the attribution header.
